[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172085,
      "id": "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5323a60c-24c8-514a-95e6-f093dd92a693",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $C(x,y)=⟨\\frac{1}{x+y+2},{x}^{2}{e}^{y}⟩$\n- **II.** $D(x,y)=⟨{y}^{3}-3,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x-y)⟩$\n- **III.** $E(x,y)=⟨x+1,y+2,x-y⟩$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $C(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y\\ne -2\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $E(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ to vectors in ${R}^{3}$ (not ${R}^{2}$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "bcf3675d-3e2b-57ef-b2bd-69bba5bd16ce",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "4ca405ff-b554-5604-8e84-61cd51ffcdfb",
