    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5a7b239d-7349-5bf9-93fc-1b23690705ec",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $V(x,y,z)=⟨5,{x}^{3}y,1-{y}^{3}⟩$\n- **II.** $U(x,y)=⟨(x+2){e}^{y},\\frac{{x}^{2}}{x-y}⟩$\n- **III.** $W(x,y)=⟨x-3{y}^{2},2-y⟩$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $V(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne y\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $W(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
