  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186172085,
      "id": "019e5a17-1aab-565b-8881-447c86a9a7ec",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Two-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "29092abe-a8d0-5bfa-bd7b-ab828ac19e99",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $B(x,y,z)=⟨-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}⟩$\n- **II.** $C(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}⟩$\n- **III.** $A(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)⟩$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "b8f049cb-3d84-515b-b888-7d855e380d7d",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
